1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,240
In 1959, Jérôme Lejeune, Marthe Gautier and Raymond

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,120
Turpin looked at dividing cells from children with

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,760
Down syndrome, and counted their chromosomes.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,440
Their cells had forty-seven, one more than the

5
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:14,800
usual forty-six.

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,400
The extra one was small, later named chromosome

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,200
twenty-one.

8
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,200
Its genes weren't faulty.

9
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:23,680
There was simply an extra copy of every

10
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:24,560
one of them.

11
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:28,160
So how can extra, missing or rearranged pieces

12
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:30,800
of chromosome change a whole organism?

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,720
A chromosomal mutation changes the structure of a

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,440
chromosome, or the number of chromosomes.

15
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,960
A point mutation, from the last video, changes

16
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:43,840
one or a few bases in one gene.

17
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:47,040
But a single chromosome carries hundreds of genes,

18
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:51,200
sometimes thousands, so a chromosomal mutation can change

19
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:52,880
many genes at once.

20
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,120
The large ones can even be seen down

21
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,160
a microscope, while a point mutation can't.

22
00:00:59,367 --> 00:01:02,326
To change shape, a chromosome usually has to

23
00:01:02,527 --> 00:01:03,286
break.

24
00:01:03,367 --> 00:01:06,646
Ionising radiation can snap both strands, and the

25
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:09,526
cell may join the broken ends back wrongly.

26
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:12,246
Mistakes in crossing over can do it too.

27
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:15,046
When two partner chromosomes line up slightly out

28
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:17,686
of step and swap pieces, one ends up

29
00:01:17,727 --> 00:01:20,006
with a stretch twice, and the other loses

30
00:01:20,087 --> 00:01:21,126
that stretch.

31
00:01:21,207 --> 00:01:23,566
So one error makes a duplication and a

32
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:25,286
deletion at the same time.

33
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,460
In a deletion, a segment is lost, along

34
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:31,460
with every gene on it.

35
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:34,020
A large deletion is often lethal, because the

36
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:36,100
cell is left with only one copy of

37
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,020
each of those genes.

38
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:41,540
In a duplication, a segment appears twice.

39
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,780
Its genes are all still there, but in

40
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:46,220
extra copies, and the number of copies of

41
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:49,540
a gene, called gene dosage, affects how much

42
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:51,220
product the cell makes.

43
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:54,020
In fruit flies, a duplicated stretch of the

44
00:01:54,100 --> 00:01:57,140
X chromosome narrows the eye into a bar.

45
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:01,806
In an inversion, a segment breaks out, flips,

46
00:02:01,887 --> 00:02:04,766
and rejoins the same chromosome back to front.

47
00:02:05,407 --> 00:02:08,126
In a translocation, a segment moves onto a

48
00:02:08,287 --> 00:02:12,206
different, non-matching chromosome, often as a swap between

49
00:02:12,287 --> 00:02:12,686
two.

50
00:02:13,407 --> 00:02:15,886
If no DNA is lost or gained, every

51
00:02:15,967 --> 00:02:18,206
gene is still there, so a carrier is

52
00:02:18,327 --> 00:02:19,246
often healthy.

53
00:02:19,567 --> 00:02:21,806
But a break can land inside a gene

54
00:02:21,887 --> 00:02:24,606
and split it, or join two genes together.

55
00:02:25,247 --> 00:02:28,286
In one leukaemia, a single blood-forming cell gets

56
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:32,126
a swap between chromosomes nine and twenty-two, joining

57
00:02:32,207 --> 00:02:34,846
two genes into one whose protein is always

58
00:02:35,087 --> 00:02:37,806
active, so white blood cells keep dividing.

59
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:41,233
There's a catch for the next generation.

60
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:43,993
A carrier's egg or sperm can get just

61
00:02:44,074 --> 00:02:46,953
one of the two swapped chromosomes, so one

62
00:02:47,034 --> 00:02:50,073
segment is missing and another is doubled.

63
00:02:50,154 --> 00:02:52,313
That's why a healthy carrier can have repeated

64
00:02:52,394 --> 00:02:55,593
miscarriages, or a child with missing or extra

65
00:02:55,674 --> 00:02:56,313
genes.

66
00:02:57,534 --> 00:03:00,413
Now the other kind, a change in chromosome

67
00:03:00,494 --> 00:03:00,893
number.

68
00:03:01,454 --> 00:03:05,613
In meiosis, partner chromosomes normally separate, one to

69
00:03:05,734 --> 00:03:06,573
each new cell.

70
00:03:07,214 --> 00:03:10,013
Sometimes a pair fails to separate, which is

71
00:03:10,174 --> 00:03:11,693
called nondisjunction.

72
00:03:12,334 --> 00:03:14,493
One gamete then gets both copies of that

73
00:03:14,574 --> 00:03:16,973
chromosome, and another gets neither.

74
00:03:17,694 --> 00:03:19,373
If the gamete with the extra copy is

75
00:03:19,454 --> 00:03:22,893
fertilised, the zygote has three copies, called a

76
00:03:22,974 --> 00:03:23,773
trisomy.

77
00:03:24,254 --> 00:03:26,813
In humans, every cell of the new organism

78
00:03:26,974 --> 00:03:29,373
then has forty-seven chromosomes.

79
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,560
That's how most cases of Down syndrome arise,

80
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,960
with three copies of chromosome twenty-one.

81
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,600
Every gene on it is normal, but there

82
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:41,080
are three copies instead of two, so the

83
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:43,400
cell makes roughly half as much again of

84
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:44,760
hundreds of products.

85
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:47,320
That's why the effects reach many parts of

86
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:47,880
the body.

87
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:50,520
It also helps explain why a baby with

88
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,400
this trisomy can survive.

89
00:03:52,680 --> 00:03:56,040
Chromosome twenty-one is the smallest, with relatively few

90
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:56,760
genes.

91
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,720
An extra copy of most other numbered chromosomes

92
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:01,800
stops development before birth.

93
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:04,560
Compare the two types.

94
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,800
Both can come from copying errors and mutagens,

95
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:11,560
but chromosomal mutations also come from breaks, faulty

96
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:14,040
crossing over and nondisjunction.

97
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:17,720
A point mutation changes one gene's base sequence,

98
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:20,920
while a chromosomal mutation changes how many genes

99
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,480
there are, their order or their position.

100
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:28,120
Chromosomal changes usually affect more genes, but bigger

101
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:29,640
isn't always worse.

102
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:32,840
A balanced inversion can do nothing, while one

103
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,280
nonsense mutation can wreck an essential protein.

104
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:39,740
Think of a chromosome as a book, and

105
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:41,500
its genes as chapters.

106
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:44,460
In a deletion, a chapter is lost.

107
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:47,500
In a duplication, it's printed twice.

108
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:51,020
In an inversion, it's printed back to front,

109
00:04:51,100 --> 00:04:53,580
and in a translocation, it's moved into a

110
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:54,460
different book.

111
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:56,620
The analogy has a limit.

112
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:59,580
An intact flipped gene is still read from

113
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:02,460
its own start, but a break inside it,

114
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:04,460
or a change to the DNA that switches

115
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:06,140
it on, can affect it.

116
00:05:07,367 --> 00:05:08,806
Two exam questions now.

117
00:05:09,287 --> 00:05:14,006
First, the diagram shows two normal, non-matching chromosomes,

118
00:05:14,087 --> 00:05:18,086
with genes as letters, and three changed versions.

119
00:05:18,167 --> 00:05:20,966
Name the chromosomal mutation in each.

120
00:05:21,287 --> 00:05:24,166
Then identify which change alters the number of

121
00:05:24,247 --> 00:05:26,006
copies of a gene in the cell, and

122
00:05:26,087 --> 00:05:27,766
explain your choice.

123
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:29,126
Pause here to answer.

124
00:05:39,534 --> 00:05:42,673
In one, E and F appear twice, so

125
00:05:42,734 --> 00:05:44,333
it's a duplication.

126
00:05:44,454 --> 00:05:47,293
In two, the segment E F G is

127
00:05:47,414 --> 00:05:49,693
back to front, an inversion.

128
00:05:49,814 --> 00:05:52,333
In three, G and H have swapped with

129
00:05:52,494 --> 00:05:54,893
P and Q on the other chromosome, a

130
00:05:55,054 --> 00:05:56,413
translocation.

131
00:05:56,574 --> 00:05:59,293
Only the duplication changes copy number.

132
00:05:59,374 --> 00:06:01,613
With its normal partner, the cell has three

133
00:06:01,694 --> 00:06:03,293
copies of E and F.

134
00:06:03,374 --> 00:06:06,893
Calling three a deletion is the classic mistake.

135
00:06:06,974 --> 00:06:09,533
G and H weren't lost, they moved.

136
00:06:10,734 --> 00:06:11,213
Second.

137
00:06:11,294 --> 00:06:14,253
Sickle cell disease is caused by a single

138
00:06:14,414 --> 00:06:16,813
base substitution in the gene for the beta

139
00:06:16,894 --> 00:06:18,093
chain of haemoglobin.

140
00:06:18,734 --> 00:06:21,453
Down syndrome is usually caused by an extra

141
00:06:21,534 --> 00:06:23,733
copy of chromosome twenty-one.

142
00:06:24,174 --> 00:06:26,573
Compare these two mutations in terms of how

143
00:06:26,654 --> 00:06:30,573
each first arises, how much DNA each changes,

144
00:06:30,694 --> 00:06:32,413
and its effect on the organism.

145
00:06:32,974 --> 00:06:34,333
Pause here to answer.

146
00:06:44,734 --> 00:06:47,893
Both change the DNA, and the phenotype.

147
00:06:48,174 --> 00:06:50,973
The substitution first came from a copying error

148
00:06:51,134 --> 00:06:54,573
or a mutagen, the trisomy from nondisjunction in

149
00:06:54,654 --> 00:06:55,533
meiosis.

150
00:06:55,774 --> 00:06:59,053
The substitution changes one base, so one amino

151
00:06:59,134 --> 00:07:00,893
acid in one protein.

152
00:07:01,134 --> 00:07:03,933
The trisomy adds a whole chromosome of normal

153
00:07:04,014 --> 00:07:06,493
genes, so hundreds of products are made in

154
00:07:06,694 --> 00:07:07,533
extra amounts.

155
00:07:08,094 --> 00:07:10,333
Saying its genes are faulty is the classic

156
00:07:10,414 --> 00:07:10,973
mistake.

157
00:07:11,294 --> 00:07:13,213
They're normal, just too many.

158
00:07:14,434 --> 00:07:17,153
So when a question shows a changed chromosome,

159
00:07:17,354 --> 00:07:19,553
compare it with the normal one, gene by

160
00:07:19,634 --> 00:07:20,113
gene.

161
00:07:20,594 --> 00:07:23,953
Is a segment missing, doubled, reversed or moved?

162
00:07:24,514 --> 00:07:26,673
Then say what that does, a change in

163
00:07:26,794 --> 00:07:29,473
copy number, or a gene broken or moved.

164
00:07:30,114 --> 00:07:32,913
For a whole extra or missing chromosome, think

165
00:07:33,154 --> 00:07:35,313
nondisjunction and dosage.

166
00:07:35,634 --> 00:07:38,753
Next, which of these mutations can be inherited.

167
00:07:39,967 --> 00:07:41,406
Here are the key notes.

168
00:07:41,647 --> 00:07:43,566
Pause the video if you need more time

169
00:07:43,647 --> 00:07:44,446
to copy them.
